# SQL Set Operations Research

SQL Set Operations are used to combine the results of two or more SELECT statements. The three main set operations are:

- UNION, INTERSECT, EXCEPT (called MINUS in Oracle)

These operations help compare data between tables and retrieve combined or matching records.

---

# 1. UNION

## Definition

UNION combines the results of two SELECT statements and removes duplicate rows.

Use UNION when you want to merge results from multiple queries and display only unique records.

## Syntax

sql SELECT column1, column2 FROM table1  UNION  SELECT column1, column2 FROM table2;

## Rules & Conditions

- Both SELECT statements must return the same number of columns.
- Corresponding columns must have compatible data types.
- Column names are taken from the first SELECT statement.
- Duplicate rows are automatically removed.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 2. INTERSECT

## Definition

INTERSECT returns only the rows that appear in both SELECT statements.

Use INTERSECT when you need common records between two result sets.

## Syntax

sql SELECT column1 FROM table1  INTERSECT  SELECT column1 FROM table2;

## Rules & Conditions

- Both queries must return the same number of columns.
- Data types must be compatible.
- Duplicate rows are removed automatically.
- Only matching rows are returned.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 3. EXCEPT / MINUS

## Definition

EXCEPT returns rows from the first query that do not exist in the second query.

Oracle uses the keyword MINUS instead of EXCEPT.

Use this operation when you need to find records that exist in one set but not another.

## Syntax (Oracle)

sql SELECT column1 FROM table1  MINUS  SELECT column1 FROM table2;

## Rules & Conditions

- Both queries must return the same number of columns.
- Data types must be compatible.
- Duplicate rows are removed automatically.
- Results come only from the first query.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# UNION vs UNION ALL

## UNION

sql SELECT department_id FROM employees  UNION  SELECT department_id FROM departments;

Characteristics:

- Removes duplicates.
- Returns only unique records.
- Requires extra processing to eliminate duplicates.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## UNION ALL

sql SELECT department_id FROM employees  UNION ALL  SELECT department_id FROM departments;

Characteristics:

- Keeps duplicates.
- Faster than UNION.
- Useful when duplicate values are required.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Summary

| Operation | Purpose | Removes Duplicates |
|------------|----------|-------------------|
| UNION | Combines results from multiple queries | Yes |
| UNION ALL | Combines results and keeps duplicates | No |
| INTERSECT | Returns common rows between queries | Yes |
| MINUS (EXCEPT) | Returns rows from first query not found in second | Yes |

## Key Rules

1. Same number of columns in each SELECT statement.
2. Corresponding columns must have compatible data types.
3. ORDER BY can only be used at the end of the entire set operation.
4. Oracle uses MINUS instead of EXCEPT.